KaizoCore patch to libretro/melonDS 66b5d2634cd0a79030562811e6e05f5532f800ba (GPL-3.0, as melonDS is), on top of
0001-0005: turning the Wi-Fi radio on no longer closes descriptor 0.

When a game powers the radio on, Wifi::Write calls Platform::MP_Init once. The Android build does not define
HAVE_WIFI, so src/libretro/platform.cpp turns socket() into NULL and setsockopt() into -1: MP_Init stored 0 in
MPSocket, passed the "MPSocket < 0" check, failed the first setsockopt and called close(0). The shipped rc35.2 arm64
library compiles MP_Init to exactly that, an unconditional close(0). On a phone fd 0 belongs to the runtime, and
Android's fdsan aborts the app ("attempted to close file descriptor 0, expected to be unowned, actually owned by
unique_fd", KaizoCore rc35.2, HeartGold/SoulSilver on a Galaxy S22+, 2026-10-05). Before 0003 the firmware carried
no Wi-Fi calibration and a game's wireless library would not start, which is the likely reason no crash came from
this path before rc34.

Now MPSocket starts as INVALID_SOCKET, not 0; without HAVE_WIFI MP_Init returns false before any socket call, so the
game sees no link partners and keeps running; MP_Init first closes a socket it opened earlier, MP_DeInit closes only
a socket MP_Init opened and forgets it after, and every check compares with INVALID_SOCKET instead of "< 0". A build
with HAVE_WIFI opens, binds and closes its own UDP socket as before. LAN_Init and LAN_DeInit touch no descriptor
without HAVE_PCAP, which the Android build does not define either.

Applied by tools/cores/build_core.py melonds; libretrodroid/cores/melonds/PINNED.txt records the result.

diff --git a/src/libretro/platform.cpp b/src/libretro/platform.cpp
index 020b0eb..1693442 100644
--- a/src/libretro/platform.cpp
+++ b/src/libretro/platform.cpp
@@ -68,7 +68,8 @@ extern char retro_base_directory[4096];
 
 #define NIFI_VER 1
 
-socket_t MPSocket;
+// No socket until MP_Init opens one: a zero here was descriptor 0, which MP_DeInit would close.
+socket_t MPSocket = INVALID_SOCKET;
 sockaddr_t MPSendAddr;
 u8 PacketBuffer[2048];
 
@@ -241,6 +242,16 @@ namespace Platform
 
    bool MP_Init()
    {
+      // Close only a socket this file opened, never a descriptor it did not.
+      if (MPSocket != INVALID_SOCKET)
+         MP_DeInit();
+
+#ifndef HAVE_WIFI
+      // Built without Wi-Fi (the Android build): there is no local multiplayer. The socket calls are stand-ins here,
+      // and socket() gave 0, which is not a socket: the first failure path closed descriptor 0, and Android's fdsan
+      // aborts the app on that, the moment a game turned the radio on. Report no link; the game finds no partners.
+      return false;
+#else
       int opt_true = 1;
       int res;
 
@@ -253,7 +264,7 @@ namespace Platform
 #endif // __WXMSW__
 
       MPSocket = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);
-      if (MPSocket < 0)
+      if (MPSocket == INVALID_SOCKET)
       {
          return false;
       }
@@ -291,12 +302,16 @@ namespace Platform
       *(u16*)&MPSendAddr.sa_data[0] = htons(7064);
 
       return true;
+#endif // HAVE_WIFI
    }
 
    void MP_DeInit()
    {
-      if (MPSocket >= 0)
+      if (MPSocket != INVALID_SOCKET)
+      {
          closesocket(MPSocket);
+         MPSocket = INVALID_SOCKET;
+      }
 
 #ifdef _WIN32
       WSACleanup();
@@ -305,7 +320,7 @@ namespace Platform
 
    int MP_SendPacket(u8* data, int len)
    {
-      if (MPSocket < 0)
+      if (MPSocket == INVALID_SOCKET)
       {
          printf("MP_SendPacket: early return (%d)\n", len);
          return 0;
@@ -331,7 +346,7 @@ namespace Platform
 
    int MP_RecvPacket(u8* data, bool block)
    {
-      if (MPSocket < 0)
+      if (MPSocket == INVALID_SOCKET)
       {
          printf("MP_RecvPacket: early return\n");
          return 0;
